This paper is the fifth in a series of papers exploring the effects that context has on memory. We focus on the issue of whether context can affect memory processes in the absence of direct external stimulation.
The issue is important because it is widely believed that the environment is critical to memory consolidation. Context is a powerful tool for altering the nature of the memory that is consolidated in memory ([@bib0045], [@sec0255], reviewed in Section 3{ref-type="sec"}).
Context effects can be found in any memory task and are found across the lifespan and in healthy as well as diseased populations. They can occur in tasks in which participants have to remember to perform a task under a specific set of conditions, such as when the task requires remembering to do a mental arithmetic task when performing an arithmetic calculation, or when participants are asked to memorize a list of words when completing a word memory test. The same context effect can also occur when a memory is assessed under different conditions that are not directly related to the test task. For example, a context condition is found when using a free recall test (e.g., Figure 1 and Table 2) or a recognition test (Table 2{#intr0007}). For these tasks, the context in question does not involve the target task or the stimuli used in that task, but instead it involves the presentation of a different task-relevant stimulus or stimulus features. Similarly, there are also context conditions in other types of tasks (Figure 1{http: //www-jstspa.uni-stuttgart.de/∼jspah/resources/images/museum-of-memories/figures/S1-A-1.eps}), such when testing the recall of different words presented in different contexts (context effect) or in multiple contexts (Figures 1 and 2A{width="8.5cm"} and S2A--D, Fig. 4{

